	"department": "HIST",
	"description": [
		"How and why did the utopian dreams of nineteenth-century European intellectuals become the ideological justifications for twentieth-century totalitarian regimes? How did theBolshevik revolution in the name of the workers and oppressed peoples lead to authoritarianism and mass terror? How were nineteenth-century romantic dreams of a unified German Volk appropriated in service to a racialized \"Volksgemeinschaft\" that fueled Nazi violence? This seminar begins with the intellectual history of utopianism. Inverted social hierarchies and re-conceptualized means of correcting or preventing injustice are favorite tropes in utopian literature and in plans for creating utopian societies. To our modern ears, this sounds progressive, no? If only we could redesign society with justice and individual human freedom in mind, everyone would benefit, right? Our task will be to consider where and how this type of thinking produces opportunities for greater equity and equality while also laying the groundwork for future exclusivity. The heart of the course will consider the two greatest perversions of utopianism in European history and their deadly, tragic consequences: Stalinism and Nazism. Through primary sources and scholarship on the \"history of everyday life,\" we will encounter a range of lived experiences of utopias in the making in the USSR and Germany."
	],
